language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ futoshiki: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The futoshiki puzzle is played on a square grid, such as 5 x 5. The objective is to place the
$ numbers 1 to 5 (or whatever the dimensions are) such that each row and each column contains each
$ of the digits 1 to 5. Some digits may be given at the start. In addition, inequality constraints
$ are also initially specified between some of the squares, such that one must be higher or lower
$ than its neighbour. These constraints must be honoured as the grid is filled out.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `futoshiki.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/futoshiki.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `futoshiki_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py futoshiki.json
$   savilerow model.eprime futoshiki_1.param -run-solver

$ n: the side of the grid, which is the number of rows of the given values (derived from the data)
given values_dim1 : int
letting n = values_dim1
$ values[r,c]: the digit given in row r, column c (rows and columns 1-based); 0 if the cell is empty
given values : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..n)] of int(0..n)
$ lt_dim1: the number of inequalities (derived from the data)
given lt_dim1 : int
$ lt[k,1..4]: inequality k says the cell in row lt[k,1], column lt[k,2] holds a smaller digit than the cell in row lt[k,3], column lt[k,4] (1-based)
given lt : matrix indexed by [int(1..lt_dim1), int(1..4)] of int(1..n)
$ grid[r,c]: the digit in row r, column c of the completed grid
find grid : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..n)] of int(1..n)

such that
$ the digits in each row are different
forAll r : int(1..n) . allDiff([grid[r,c] | c : int(1..n)]),
$ the digits in each column are different
forAll c : int(1..n) . allDiff([grid[r,c] | r : int(1..n)]),
$ the given digits are kept
forAll r : int(1..n) . forAll c : int(1..n) . (values[r,c] > 0 -> grid[r,c] = values[r,c]),
$ each inequality holds
forAll k : int(1..lt_dim1) . grid[lt[k,1], lt[k,2]] < grid[lt[k,3], lt[k,4]]
